<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tamil-brahmi">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamil-Brahmi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamil-Brahmi is a variant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> used to write the Tamil language from around the 3rd or 2nd century BCE. It is found in inscriptions in caves used by Jain monks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and scratched on pottery, including finds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> ports in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It is the ancestor of the later Tamil and Vatteluttu scripts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi.jpg" alt="Tamil-Brahmi" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Symphonicallyu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tamil_Brahmi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its earliest records are short inscriptions in <b>Tamil-Brahmi</b>, a variant of the Brahmi script, and earlier dates have been claimed: burial urns found at Adichanallur in 2004, dating from at least 696 BCE, carry writing in Tamil-Brahmi, and a sample from Keezhadi has been claimed to date to…</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the layers of a site, sherds with symbols lie lowest, then sherds mixing symbols with Brahmi or <b>Tamil-Brahmi</b>, and at the top Brahmi or Tamil-Brahmi alone.</blockquote><a href="/culture/south-indian-megalithic-culture">South Indian megalithic culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Tamil was written from about 300 BCE in <b>Tamil-Brahmi</b>; the oldest Malay, the Kedukan Bukit inscription of 683 near Palembang, in a Pallava script; and Pali, which also has no script of its own, in the script of each Theravada land, Sinhala, Mon-Burmese, Khmer, Thai since 1893 and…</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tamil-Brahmi</b> inscriptions at Pugalur name kings of the Irumporai branch.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-cheras">Early Cheras</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tamil-Brahmi</b> inscriptions record gifts to Jain monks.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-pandyas">Early Pandyas</a></li></ul></section>
